### 2.4 \(R_{\rm dAu}\)

While Au+Au collisions may produce QGP, it is important to verify that observed modification from \(p+p\) data is the result of interactions in QGP and not interactions with a cold nucleus. Asymmetric collisions of protons or deuterons with a heavy nuclei are unlikely to produce QGP in the way that heavy-ion collisions do so that any modification from \(p\)+\(p\) collisions would be due to cold nuclear matter (CNM) effects. The nuclear modification factor, \(R_{\rm dAu}\)(Eq. 2.1), shows how much net modification there is for \(d\)+Au spectra relative to \(p\)+\(p\) spectra as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\). \(R_{\rm dAu}\) uses \(N_{\rm bin}\) scaling so that if there is no net nuclear modification of \(d\)+Au spectra relative to \(p\)+\(p\) spectra, \(R_{\rm dAu}\) equates to unity. Different centrality bins may be determined from event activity and \(\langle N_{\rm bin}\rangle\) values estimated from a Glauber Monte Carlo simulation.

Figure 2.5 shows \(R_{\rm dAu}\) for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,\)GeV for inclusive centrality as well as the 0-20% most central events, and for pions and protons as well as charged hadrons [53]. There is significant enhancement for \(2<p_{\rm T}<7\,\)GeV/c for minimum bias3 produced charged hadrons. Protons are more enhanced than pions and particles from the 0-20% highest

activity collisions are more enhanced than those from minimum bias collisions. Previous measurements by Cronin \(etal\). of assymetric collisions at lower \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\mathrm{NN}}}}\) also found these effects and so the measured enhancement has come to be called the "Cronin effect" or "Cronin enhancement" [54, 55, 56]. These CNM enhancement effects are the result of having a single heavy ion in the collision. Systems with two heavy ions would likely have stronger CNM enhancement effects [57, 58]. If this enhancement is due to \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\)-broadening, then the enhancement would be stronger for steeper spectra. Suppression effects in heavy-ion collisions would have to be stronger than CNM enhancement effects in order to result in net suppression in \(R_{\mathrm{AA}}\) or \(R_{\mathrm{CP}}\).

### 2.5 \(R_{\mathrm{CP}}\)

The nuclear modification factor, \(R_{\mathrm{CP}}\) (Eq. 1.2), shows how much net modification there is for central spectra relative to peripheral spectra as a function of \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\). \(R_{\mathrm{CP}}\) uses \(N_{\mathrm{bin}}\) scaling so that if there is no net nuclear modification of central spectra relative to peripheral spectra then \(R_{\mathrm{CP}}\) would be unity. This is based off the assumption that the high-\(Q^{2}\) part of Au+Au collisions may be modeled as \(N_{\mathrm{bin}}\) independent incoherent \(p+p\)-like collisions. Different centrality bins may be chosen for the central and peripheral spectra. The most central spectra are expected to be subject to the most modification, and the most peripheral spectra are expected to be the most _p_+_p_-like.

Sample fully corrected \(R_{\rm CP}\) results are shown in Fig. 2.6 for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,\)GeV with two different peripheral bins for the reference [45]. The 60-80% centrality class was found to have little net high-\(p_{\rm T}\) nuclear modification from a _p_+_p_ baseline, as shown in Fig. 2.7[45]. There may still have been some high-\(p_{\rm T}\) suppression, but if so this was countered by enhancement effects. While unmodified high-\(p_{\rm T}\) hadrons are expected to exhibit \(N_{\rm bin}\) scaling, the less rare particles at very low-\(p_{\rm T}\) are expected to scale with \(N_{\rm part}\). The theory curves correspond to pQCD calculations, with or without shadowing and Cronin effects included, as well as an extension of a saturation model to high momentum transfer.

