### 5.2 Tracking Efficiency

The \(p_{\rm T}\), centrality, and species dependent tracking efficiencies in the TPC were determined by embedding Monte Carlo tracks, modified by a GEANT [85, 86] simulation of STAR, into real events for each energy [87]. The embedding was flat in -1 \(<\eta<\) 1 and flat in 0 \(<p_{\rm T}<\) 5 GeV/\(c\) in minimum bias events, with \(\sim\)500k embedded tracks per collision energy and species. The efficiency was defined to be the fraction of embedded Monte Carlo tracks that, after event reconstruction, were matched to reconstructed tracks that passed track quality cuts. These efficiencies were determined with respect to \(p_{\rm T}\) and in slices of \(Mult\), as for the feeddown correction. The correction was determined with respect to the Monte Carlo \(p_{\rm T}\) and since it is determined in narrow \(p_{\rm T}\) bins no \(p_{\rm T}\) weighting is needed. These \(p_{\rm T}\)-dependent efficiencies, for each species and \(Mult\) slice in the BES, were fit using, Eq. 5.3; while the efficiency corrected single species spectra were fit with Eq. 5.5.

\[\epsilon(p_{\rm T})={\rm p}0\cdot e^{-({\rm p}1/p_{\rm T})^{p2}} \tag{5.3}\]

