### \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\)

Fits to the charged hadron \(p_{\rm T}\)-spectra can be used to determine \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\),

\[\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle=\frac{\int_{0}^{\infty}p_{\rm T}\frac{d^{2}N}{dp_{\rm T }d\eta}(p_{\rm T})dp_{\rm T}}{\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{d^{2}N}{dp_{\rm T}d\eta}(p _{\rm T})dp_{\rm T}}, \tag{7.1}\]

for the various centralities and collision energies. Figure 7.2 shows these results with statistical errors only. An exponential fit at low-\(p_{\rm T}\) was used for the extrapolation to \(p_{\rm T}\) = 0 GeV/c. The fit function was only used to extrapolate to \(p_{\rm T}\) = 0 GeV/c with the bin contents above \(p_{\rm T}\) = 0.2 GeV/c summed together and then added to the integral of the fit at low-\(p_{\rm T}\). The systematic uncertainties due to the feed-down correction, the tracking efficiency, centrality determination, choice of fit function, and due to the fit's low-\(p_{\rm T}\) extrapolation were not evaluated here. These uncertainties would be highly correlated with \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) and centrality.

\(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) increases monotonically as a function of \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) for 60-80% peripheral collisions, but has a minimum in \(11.5<\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}<27\) GeV for 0-5% central collisions. Because the systematic uncertainties in this measurement are highly correlated with \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\), this non-monotonic trend is likely significant. \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) increases monotonically with \(N_{\rm part}\) for a given \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\).

