## Chapter 8 Discussion

This chapter dives into possible physics interpretations for the results from the previous chapter. The sections are ordered in the same sequence as in the previous chapter.

Figure 8.1: Charged hadron 0-5% central \(p_{\rm T}\)-spectra in \(|\eta|<0.5\) is shown for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\,\)GeV Au+Au collisions with an exponential fit.

### 8.1 Spectra

The corrected \(p_{\rm T}\)-spectra at low \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) are well described by exponentials, as shown in Fig. 8.1 for 0-5% central Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV. However, as the \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) is increased a high-\(p_{\rm T}\) power-law tail develops. This motivates using a two component model to describe the spectra [52]:

\[\frac{d^{2}N}{2\pi p_{\rm T}d\eta dp_{\rm T}}=A_{e}\cdot exp(-p_{\rm T}/T_{e}) +\frac{A}{(1+\frac{p_{\rm T}^{2}}{2\cdot n})^{n}}, \tag{8.1}\]

where \(A_{e}\), \(T_{e}\), \(A\), \(T\), and \(n\) are the fit parameters. The only limits imposed on the fits were that \(A_{e}\) and \(A\) should be positive. The exponential term describes a simple thermal system at low \(p_{\rm T}\) while the power-law term describes mid to high-\(p_{\rm T}\) fragmentation. Replacing the exponential term with a Blast-Wave formula [91] would provide a more physically accurate description of the radially expanding emission source, but the simpler exponential function is sufficient to motivate particle production from at least two separate processes at higher \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\).

Figure 8.2 shows fits to 0-5% central charged hadron \(p_{\rm T}\)-spectra for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 and 62.4 GeV using this two component model. Although the 7.7 GeV data were previously

Figure 8.2: Corrected charged hadron \(p_{\rm T}\)-spectra for 0-5% central Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 (a) and 62.4 GeV (b) with two component fits. The exponential contribution to the fit is shown with the green curve while the power-law contribution is shown with the blue curve.

well constrained by an exponential alone, the two component fit favors a large contribution from the power-law. Fits such as these can be improved by constraining the parameters with other data. For example, the exponential slope parameter in these fits could be constrained with exponential fits in the low-\(p_{\rm T}\) region before performing the two component fit over the entire \(p_{\rm T}\) range. Also, the various parameters in this fit are related to physical quantities and would be expected to have certain trends as a function of \(N_{\rm part}\). For example, the exponential slope parameters are related to the temperatures of the emission sources. These "temperatures" would be expected to increase monotonically with \(N_{\rm part}\). This is clearly the case in Fig. 7.1(a).

### \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\)

Non-monotonic trends with \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) are very exciting for some observables where they are taken as possible signatures for a critical point or a softest point in the equation of state. This means we want to consider carefully the physics that might drive this observable.

One might expect \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) to increase monotonically with both \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) and \(N_{\rm part}\). This would be driven by hydrodynamic flow, especially radial flow, boosting particles to higher \(p_{\rm T}\).

Figure 8.3: \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) for identified species in the BES [89].

The higher phase space density in central collisions and at higher \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) might also be expected to increase the contribution of coalescence to hadronization, which would also cause \(\langle p_{\rm T}\ \rangle\) to increase with \(N_{\rm part}\) and \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\). The non-monotonic shape versus \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) for high \(N_{\rm part}\) is somewhat surprising then. Recall however that these are inclusive charged hadron spectra. The \(p/\pi\) ratios can vary with \(N_{\rm part}\) and each species can be expected to have a different \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) for a given \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) and \(N_{\rm part}\) with \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle_{p}>\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle_{\pi}\) as shown in Fig. 8.3[89]. The non-monotonic behavior for the charged hadrons at low \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) and high \(N_{\rm part}\) is then driven by the increase in \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle_{p}\) with \(N_{\rm part}\) and the high \(p/\pi\) at low \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\), as shown in Fig. 8.4[89]. The \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) follows the expected trends for the individual species and it is just the particle ratios that drive the non-monotonic trends in charged hadron \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) for central collisions at low \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\).

### \(R_{\rm CP}\)

High-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadrons are suppressed in 0-5% central Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 62.4 GeV relative to 60-80% peripheral collisions. They are less suppressed than in \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 200 GeV data and are energy ordered with suppression giving way to enhancement between \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 39 and 27 GeV. This means that suppression of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadrons, as a signature for the formation of QGP in heavy-ion collisions, "turns off" between \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 39 and 27 GeV. However the strong enhancement at lower \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) suggests that processes that lead to enhancement are competing with partonic energy-loss so that this signature has a reduced sensitivity to the actual minimum energy required to form a QGP.

The strength of the enhancement increases with decreasing \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) until the lowest collision energy in the RHIC BES where \(R_{\rm CP}\) increases exponentially with \(p_{\rm T}\). This is driven by the fact that the spectra at this energy are themselves well described by exponentials with different temperature parameters so that their ratios give another exponential. This may mean that the spectra are dominated by a thermalized bulk at all \(p_{\rm T}\) for this energy. Because the spectra at lower \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) fall much more steeply than at higher \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\), there are fewer high-\(p_{\rm T}\) partons to fragment down to lower \(p_{\rm T}\). It may be that with a larger \(p_{\rm T}\) reach, from additional data, the \(R_{\rm CP}\) at more of these \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) would eventually turn over as \(p_{\rm T}\) bins were reached with increasing relative contributions from fragmentation versus bulk physics. While there are several sources of enhancement that come from interactions with cold nuclear matter or from hadronic interactions, these sources may not be sufficient to describe the degree of enhancement observed at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV.

Another source of enhancement would be coalescence. Combining low-\(p_{\rm T}\) partons into a higher \(p_{\rm T}\) hadron would lead to enhancement, and it may be that much of the enhancement at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) =7.7 GeV derives from this source. If so then this would be another signature for the production of QGP. One way to test this idea would be to look at nuclear modification factors from collision energies lower than \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV where we do not expect to be forming a QGP to see if the nuclear modification factor decreases.

It might be useful to do this for negative charged hadrons only to reduce complications from changing particle ratios with larger enhancement effects for protons.

### 8.4 \(S_{loss}\)

Here we are measuring the shift in the spectra to higher or lower \(p_{\rm T}\) rather than taking ratios at fixed \(p_{\rm T}\). This is useful because the spectra decrease as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) faster at lower \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) so that the same shift in \(p_{\rm T}\) results in a larger enhancement or suppression of the modified spectra relative to the reference spectra. A decrease in \(p_{\rm T}\) at high-\(p_{\rm T}\) is expected to be driven by partonic energy-loss. At lower \(p_{\rm T}\) there may be several processes that would lead to enhancement. These include \(p_{\rm T}\)-broadening in cold nuclear matter, radial flow, and coalescence.

Anywhere that \(R_{\rm CP}\) is greater than unity, \(S_{loss}\) is negative as "energy" was gained rather than lost. This may actually be driven by additional particle production rather than by shifts in \(p_{\rm T}\) so \(R_{\rm CP}\) and \(S_{loss}\) are complementary measurements from the same spectra under different assumptions.

Interestingly, the magnitude of the \(S_{loss}\) turns out to be energy ordered with \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\) GeV exhibiting larger increases in \(p_{\rm T}\) than \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=11.5\) GeV. This may mean that a larger fraction of the particles at each \(p_{\rm T}\) originate from coalescence for 7.7 GeV collisions than for 11.5 GeV collisions. It may also be driven by a change in the particle ratios between the two energies with 7.7 GeV collisions having more protons than 11.5 GeV collisions and protons being shifted more by radial flow and other processes.

By measuring \(S_{loss}\) for negative charged hadrons, which are pion dominated for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\) and 11.5 GeV, it is possible to disentangle whether the particle ratios or the relative contributions of coalescence and fragmentation to the hadronization process drive the larger \(p_{\rm T}\) shift measured for 7.7 GeV collisions. The measurement supports an increase in the fraction of the hadronization for a given \(p_{\rm T}\) in \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\) GeV collisions relative to 11.5 GeV collisions. The difference in \(S_{loss}\) between \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=\)7.7 and 11.5 GeV is smaller for negative charged hadrons than for inclusive charged hadrons meaning that the higher fraction of protons in \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\) GeV collisions is responsible for some of the enhancement at this energy. However negative charged hadrons at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\) GeV still have a larger \(p_{\rm T}\) shift than negative charged hadrons in 11.5 GeV collisions. This may be driven by the fraction of the hadrons in each \(p_{\rm T}\) bin that originate from lower \(p_{\rm T}\) partons coalescing to hadrons. These lower \(p_{\rm T}\) partons would have larger \(p_{\rm T}\) gains from radial flow in the partonic phase and the final hadron would gain \(p_{\rm T}\) from all of its constituents rather than just one.

### \(Y\)

By investigating \(N_{\rm bin}\)-scaled yields as a function of \(N_{\rm part}\) the modifications due to enhancement and suppression are partially disentangled. For low \(N_{\rm part}\), enhancement effects increase faster than suppression effects as a function of \(N_{\rm part}\) for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}<200\) GeV. However the 62.4 GeV data quickly turns over and suppression effects increase faster than enhancement effects for most \(N_{\rm part}\). As the collision energy is reduced, you have to go more and more central before a centrality is reached where suppression effects increase faster than enhancement effects. Using this method, evidence for partonic energy-loss is found for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\geq 14.5\) GeV.

Using identified \(\pi^{\pm}\), \(K^{\pm}\), \(p\), and \(\bar{p}\) spectra from the BES energies [90], \(Y(N_{\rm part})\) for each of these species is constructed. The \(p_{\rm T}\) reach is more limited for these identified species. Results for \(2.4<p_{\rm T}<2.6\) GeV/c are shown in Fig. 8.5. The inclusive systematic uncertainties on the spectra from Evan Sangaline's thesis are added in quadrature with the systematic uncertainties on \(N_{\rm bin}\). \(Y(N_{\rm part})\) for protons and anti-protons is nearly flat as a function of centrality for \(N_{\rm part}\geq 150\). Kaons are enhanced relative to pions. \(\pi^{+}\) and \(\pi^{-}\) are similar with \(Y(N_{\rm part})\) exhibiting suppression for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\geq 19.6\) GeV and nearly flat for 11.5 GeV.

The mean impact parameter in 0-5% collisions for these energies is 2.3\(\pm\).1 fm while in 10-20% collisions the mean impact parameter is 5.6\(\pm\).2 fm. This suggests that the mean in-medium pathlength is significantly longer in 0-5% collisions. So the suppression of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) inclusive charged hadrons and negative charged hadrons in 0-5% central Au+Au collisions relative to 10-20% central collisions is consistent with pathlength dependent partonic energy-loss.

This measurement of \(Y(N_{\rm part})\) motivated constructing an \(R_{\rm CP}\) where 10-20% central data is used as the reference, shown in Fig. 7.10. In this case we see that the suppression of the 0-5% data was not a coincidence of the chosen \(p_{\rm T}\) range and is in fact a robust measurement of suppression.

Figure 8.5: Identified \(p\), \(\bar{p}\), \(K^{\pm}\), and \(\pi^{\pm}\)\(Y(N_{\rm part})\) in \(|y|<0.25\) is shown for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\), 11.5, 19.6, 27, 39, and 62.4 GeV. The error bands are the quadrature sum of the uncertainties on \(N_{\rm bin}\), from the feed-down correction, from the tracking efficiency, and from the particle identification procedure [90].

## Chapter 9 Modeling Heavy-Ion Collisions

The collection of physical processes that modify \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra are in reality too complicated to be described by simple fits to spectra. More physically accurate models describe initial state and cold nuclear matter effects, produce partons at all phases of the collision, model a partonic phase that starts out-of-equilibrium, correctly handle the modification of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) partons interacting with the medium, hadronize the partons through the correct combination of fragmentation at high-\(p_{\rm T}\) and coalescence at low-\(p_{\rm T}\), describe hadronic interactions up to freeze-out, and decay short lived particles at the appropriate times throughout the collision evolution.

Models with these components provide a possible method for disentangling partonic energy-loss from the various enhancement effects in heavy-ion collisions. If a model can reproduce many observables over a broad range of kinematics then the model likely gets the relative contributions of each source of modification correct. By comparing the data to the model with jet quenching absent one could obtain an estimate for the modification due to jet-quenching. Even if the model does not describe jet-quenching itself very well, if the model can be validated in regions with minimal partonic energy-loss then a comparison with the model where the model's partonic energy-loss processes were turned off could still provide evidence for suppression in the data relative to such a model.

\(p_{\rm T}\) spectra are constructed from charged hadrons that are effected by every phase of heavy-ion collisions. Models that attempt to quantitatively describe data must accurately describe initial state effects, the pre-equilibrium phase, partonic transport, hadronization, hadronic rescattering, and final-state decays. To describe \(p_{\rm T}\) integrated yields only requires getting the net effects for the bulk right, but differential yields require getting the physics at every phase and in every kinematic region quantitatively right. This is a tall order. As Bylinkin, Chernyavskaya, and Rostovtsev said in a recent paper on two component models for charged particle production, "_there is no single theoretical approach that can simultaneously describe both low-\(p_{\rm T}\) and high-\(p_{\rm T}\) hadron production_" [52]. Models that simulate heavy-ion collisions use many components to describe the different kinematic regions and phases of medium production and evolution. These models often focus on getting a particular type of physics right, so they may not do well at simulating physics that was not the focus of the model as it was being constructed. For example, models that include hydrodynamic phases in order to describe the evolution of low-\(p_{\rm T}\) correlations in the bulk may not do well at describing high-\(p_{\rm T}\) processes.

Monte Carlo event generators use different, sometimes interchangeable, models to describe each component of a heavy-ion collision. They typically begin with a set of randomized initial conditions. For the models discussed in this chapter, those initial conditions

Figure 9.1: The correlation of impact parameter with charged multiplicity in \(|\eta|<0.5\) at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=14.5\) GeV from UrQMD.

are provided event-by-event using a Glauber Monte Carlo. From there, each model includes different components to describe particle production and evolution. Theorists often select on centrality classes using impact parameters, but experimentally the centrality selection is typically done with event activity measures in various rapidity ranges. The impact parameter is anti-correlated with charged multiplicity at mid-rapidity, as shown in Fig. 9.1 for UrQMD generated events at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 14.5 GeV, but there is some spread in multiplicity values for a fixed impact parameter. These different methods of selecting centrality may introduce some bias, so for these comparisons the final state charged multiplicity in \(|\eta|\) \(<\) 0.5 was used to select centrality in the models so as to be consistent with the experiment. The models that appear in this chapter are HIJING, AMPT, and UrQMD. They are run with their default parameter values.

Figure 9.2: HIJING generated charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) in \(|\eta|\)\(<\) 0.5 are shown for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7, 11.5, 19.6, 27, 39, 62.4, and 200 GeV for 0-5% over 60-80% centrality bins.

### Hijing

Each model has different versions as they are improved and tuned to new data. The Heavy Ion Jet INteraction Generator (HIJING) version 1.383 [92] starts from a Glauber Monte Carlo with impact parameter dependent shadowing and merges multi-string phenomenology at low \(p_{\rm T}\) with minijet production for \(p_{\rm T}>2\,\)GeV/c [93]. Minijets are produced using the same methods as the PYTHIA _p_+_p_ event generator with initial and final state radiation. Minijet1 partonic energy loss is then modeled using the string geometry to get an in-medium path-length. This path-length is multiplied by a fixed estimate of the in-medium energy-loss per unit length, \(dE/dl=1\,\)GeV/c/fm for a quark jet and \(2\,\)GeV/c/fm for a gluon jet, to determine the total energy lost by the minijet to the medium. Energy and momentum are conserved in this model by transferring them to the strings closest to the minijets' trajectories. There is no explicit partonic or hadronic phase in this model and so it would not be expected to reproduce bulk signatures at low-\(p_{\rm T}\). It does include a "\(p_{\rm T}\) kick" modification for quarks and diquarks at string end points which models \(p_{\rm T}\) broadening at low \(p_{\rm T}\).

Figure 9.3: Ratios of charged hadron \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra from HIJING to those from data for 0-5% central (a) and 60-80% peripheral collisions (b) for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\), 11.5, 19.6, 27, 39, 62.4, and 200 GeV.

Figure 9.2 shows the charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) for 0-5%/60-80% centralities generated by HIJING in \(|\eta|<0.5\) for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\), 11.5, 19.6, 27, 39, 62.4, and 200 GeV. As in data, there is a strong \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) dependence to the amount of enhancement or suppression observed at high \(p_{\rm T}\). However, the ratios of the \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra from data to those from HIJING, shown in Fig. 9.3, reveal that the \(p_{\rm T}\) dependence of the spectra are not well described. Still, this relatively simple model does a remarkably good job of qualitatively describing the particle production over a large range of \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\). Its simplicity means that it does not require many computational resources, making this an excellent model for simulating detector response.

### 9.2 AMPT

A Multi-Phase Transport (AMPT) model [94] has two major versions for modeling the partonic phase and hadronization. The default version 1.21 and string melting version 2.21 both start with HIJING initial conditions and use Zhang's Parton Cascade (ZPC) for modeling partonic scatterings, although the default version only runs the minijet partons through ZPC before recombining them with their parent strings. The default version of AMPT then uses the same LUND string fragmentation model for hadronization as HIJING used, but with differently tuned parameters. The string melting version of the model first breaks up the strings into partons, runs these with the partons from minijets through ZPC, and then uses a basic coalescence model for hadronization. Then both versions of the model use A Relativistic Transport model for hadronic scatterings. AMPT is tuned to Pb+Pb data at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=17.3\) GeV. \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra from _p_+_p_ collisions are well described at low \(p_{\rm T}\) by AMPT, as are rapidity densities for a variety of collision systems [94]. AMPT with string melting on is designed to describe the physics of the bulk and so it does well at describing two-particle correlations [94].

The power of transport models is that they can describe pre-equilibrium and near equilibrium systems that do not satisfy the equilibrium assumptions required for hydrodynamic and thermal models [94].

#### String-Melting On

The coalescence version of the model might not be expected to accurately describe the high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadron spectra where fragmentation should be the dominant hadronization process. In fact using the default parameters results in a \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) dependence for \(R_{\rm CP}\), shown in Fig. 9.4, that is similar to what was measured in data. However the ratios of the spectra from the model to the data shown in Fig. 9.5 reveal that the string melting version of the model with default parameters underpredicts the data at \(p_{\rm T}=3\,\)GeV/c by an order of magnitude.

Figure 9.4: AMPT generated charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) in \(|\eta|<0.5\) for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\), 11.5, 19.6, 27, 39, 62.4, and 200 GeV for 0-5% over 60-80% centrality bins are shown from the string melting version of the model.

#### String-Melting Off

The default version of the model would be expected to do a better job of describing the high-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) kinematics of heavy-ion collisions where fragmentation should dominate the hadronization processes. Instead Fig. 9.6 displays minimal \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\mathrm{NN}}}}\) dependence for high-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\)\(R_{\mathrm{CP}}\). The ratios of the spectra from the model to the data are shown in Fig. 9.7. The spectra are closer to the data than for the string melting version of the model, but still underpredict the data. This may be due, in part, to the different Lund spitting function parameters used in the default version of AMPT from those used in HIJING and PYTHIA.

To estimate the effect of running with AMPT Lund spliting function parameters rather than those used in HIJING, HIJING was run with both set of values. The ratios of the central and peripheral spectra with default parameters to those with AMPT's parameters are shown in Fig. 9.8. Clearly, AMPT's values for these parameters reduce its high-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) yields and shifts it further from the data.

Figure 9.5: Ratios of charged hadron \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) spectra from the string melting version of AMPT to those from data for 0-5% central (a) and 60-80% peripheral collisions (b) for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\mathrm{NN}}}}\) = 7.7, 11.5, 19.6, 27, 39, 62.4, and 200 GeV.

