### 9.4 Summary

Each model discussed in this chapter was designed to reproduce the results of heavy-ion collisions. While each can reproduce many physics results they all fail to reproduce centrality dependent \(p_{\rm T}\)-spectra over a broad range of \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) using their default parameter settings. They generally underpredict the spectra at high-\(p_{\rm T}\). The magnitude of this discrepancy is concealed when the ratios of spectra from different centralities are taken for \(R_{\rm CP}\) predictions. HIJING and AMPT with string melting on both exhibit a strong \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) dependence to the enhancement of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) that qualitatively agrees with the data. However, HIJING over predicts the enhancement of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadrons at low \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) and underpredicts the suppression at high \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\). AMPT, on the other hand, underpredicts the enhancement at low \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\). AMPT with string melting off and UrQMD fail to qualitatively describe the \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) dependence of charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) at high \(p_{\rm T}\).

