## Chapter 10 Conclusions and Outlook

The first purpose of this dissertation is to test the prediction that high-\(p_{\rm T}\) suppression of charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) should "turn off" at sufficiently low \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) if it is really a signature of QGP formation. The second purpose is to set a limit from high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadrons for the minimum \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) needed to form a QGP. Although high-\(p_{\rm T}\)\(R_{\rm CP}\) is below unity for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=39\,\)GeV, only high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadrons at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=62.4\,\)GeV are suppressed when scaling uncertainties are accounted for. In carrying out this dissertation research it was determined that large enhancement effects are competing with the suppression effect that we want to measure, i.e. partonic energy-loss in a QGP. In order to advance this research beyond the measurement of net suppression of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadrons in \(N_{\rm bin}\)-scaled central collisions relative to peripheral collisions, new methods and observables are developed.

One method is to measure suppression relative to models that include the various enhancement effects. However these models are found to need more development and fine-tuning to provide the level of precision needed for this comparison.

Another method to improve the sensitivity to QGP is to use hadrons that are less sensitive to enhancement effects. Because protons have been found to be more affected by enhancement effects than pions, using high-\(p_{\rm T}\) pions as probes for suppression improvessensitivity to partonic energy-loss. Particle identification becomes increasingly difficult at high \(p_{\rm T}\) though, so an alternative is to only look at negatively charged hadrons. At low \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\), few anti-protons are produced in heavy-ion collisions so that negatively charged hadrons mostly consist of \(\pi^{-}\) mesons. Negative charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) is below inclusive charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) for all \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) in the BES. High-\(p_{\rm T}\) negative charged hadron \(R_{\rm CP}\) is below unity for \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\geq 27\,\)GeV, however it is still the case that only the 62.4 GeV \(R_{\rm CP}\) is significantly suppressed.

Because the physical process that we are trying to describe is a shift in \(p_{\rm T}\) rather than the formation or annihilation of hadrons at a particular \(p_{\rm T}\) the fractional energy-loss observable, \(S_{loss}\), is more closely linked to the physics of interest. \(R_{\rm CP}\) is sensitive to the shapes of the spectra so that the same shift in \(p_{\rm T}\) will cause a larger change in \(R_{\rm CP}\) from steep, low-\(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) data than from less steep, high-\(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) data. Surprisingly, the enhancement in \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=7.7\,\)GeV \(R_{\rm CP}\) is driven by a shift of \(p_{\rm T}\) for central spectra relative to peripheral spectra that is even larger than the shift from higher\(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\). The possibility that this is solely caused by particle ratios and larger enhancement of protons than pions is once again ruled out by measuring \(S_{loss}\) for negative charged hadrons, although larger enhancement of protons did contribute significantly. Two possible explanations for the remaining larger \(p_{\rm T}\) enhancement of negative charged hadrons in \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV data are presented here. One possibility is that there is only more _net_ enhancement at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV. If the combined enhancement effects provide the same or less \(p_{\rm T}\) boost to hadrons in central \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV collisions than they do in 11.5 GeV collisions then this difference must be driven by larger energy-loss at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 11.5 GeV. This would be evidence for QGP formation at 11.5 GeV. The other possibility is that the difference is driven by the fraction of hadrons originating from coalescence versus fragmentation. The steeper spectra at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV reduces the number of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) partons that can produce hadrons for a particular intermediate to high-\(p_{\rm T}\) bin. If a QGP is formed, however, then coalescing low-\(p_{\rm T}\) partons can produce intermediate to high-\(p_{\rm T}\) hadrons. Going from \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 11.5 GeV to 7.7 GeV, a larger fraction of the hadrons at a given \(p_{\rm T}\) would originate from coalescence. Enhancement effects on each parton would contribute to the \(p_{\rm T}\) shift of the final hadron leading to larger \(p_{\rm T}\) shifts for hadrons in the \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV central collisions than in 11.5 GeV collisions. If this is the correct interpretation of these results then it provides evidence for QGP formation in \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 7.7 GeV central Au+Au collisions.

A new method for disentangling enhancement and suppression effects is developed in this dissertation. That is to measure the \(N_{\rm part}\) dependence of \(N_{\rm bin}\)-scaled high-\(p_{\rm T}\) yields. Because enhancement and suppression effects can have different centrality dependence it is possible to measure the net modification of the most central data relative to a centrality bin that already includes enhancement effects. Both \(Y(N_{\rm part})\) and a nuclear modification factor using 10-20% central spectra as the reference provide evidence from high-\(p_{\rm T}\) charged hadrons of QGP formation in 0-5% central, \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 14.5 GeV collisions.

Taken together, these measurements provide strong evidence for QGP formation down to \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 14.5 GeV. There is also one measurement that is consistent with QGP formation in 0-5% central Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 11.5 GeV.

\(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 19.6 GeV _d_+Au events collected in 2016 provide a reference for cold nuclear matter effects at a moderate BES collision energy. Making spectra from these events, as well as the Au+Au spectra in this thesis, available will provide important constraints for models.

Triggered dihadron correlations would provide a complimentary high-\(p_{\rm T}\) partonic energy-loss signature. Such a measurement may require more statistics than are currently available for the lower energies in the BES in order to separate the hard component from the background. This is suggested by Fig. 10.1 where the nearside, \(\Delta\eta\)=0 and \(\Delta\phi\) =0, jet structure that is prominent at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 200 GeV vanishes with current statistics at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 39 GeV. \(\gamma\)-hadron, \(\gamma\)-jet, jet-hadron, or hadron-jet correlations would provide alternative means of studying particles produced in hard scatterings at BES energies.

The measurements in this dissertation may be improved with the extended \(p_{\rm T}\) reach from the higher statistics in BES II, the second phase of the RHIC BES that is scheduled to take data in 2019-2020. STAR has extended the RHIC kinematics by installing a fixed target which extends the lower limit of the heavy ion collision energy accessible at STAR below \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=3.9\,\)GeV [96]. Data at these lower energies will also be collected during BES II. Au+Au collisions at this low of \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) should not produce a QGP. Thus, they will serve as a reference for many of the bulk measurements as well as the observables discussed in this thesis.

